# DOMAIN 1 — GLOBAL FLASHPOINTS

## Purpose of this domain
Track external events that can inject pressure into domestic, economic, or civil systems with little warning — especially where escalation reduces reaction time for households.

This domain is upstream.  
Most of it never alerts.  
Its value is in early causality, not immediacy.

---

## 1.1 What still belongs (validated)

These remain relevant:
- Middle East escalation vectors
- Taiwan / South China Sea
- Russia / NATO boundary stress
- Energy chokepoints (e.g., Hormuz, Red Sea)
- Alliance signaling and kinetic spillover

Structure holds.

---

## 1.2 What changed (re-baseline)

The risk is no longer “war starts.”

It is:
- containment failure  
- miscalculation  
- spillover into trade, energy, cyber, or travel  
- security framing used to justify domestic measures  

So we stop watching for invasion headlines and start watching for secondary effects.

---

## 1.3 Triggers

### 🔴 ALERT-ELIGIBLE (rare, stacked only)

These do **not** alert alone.  
They alert only when they stack with **Domain 3 (Economic)** or **Domain 2 (Domestic)** impacts.

| Trigger | P | I | O | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Direct attack on U.S. or allied assets | L | H | H | Watch escalation chain |
| Energy chokepoint disruption | M | H | M | Shipping routes, Red Sea, Hormuz |
| Alliance-wide mobilization language | L | H | M | Not rhetoric, policy |
| Cyber operations affecting civilian infrastructure | M | H | M | Power, transport |
| Rapid sanctions with domestic spillover | M | M | H | Payments, fuel |

**Alert rule:**  
👉 Only if domestic access, cost, movement, or enforcement is affected within days.

